# UNVERIFIED — pending debrief
#
# `s @ s` and `s @ s + s`: the order of the two refcount operations around a store is
# the whole rule. A store must incref the NEW value before decrefing the old (panel
# 021 rule 3) — reversed, a self-assignment frees the buffer and then increfs a dead
# one, which is a use-after-free that prints correctly at `-O0` because the bytes are
# still there.
#
# The loop is the shape every string-building function in the self-hosted compiler
# will have, and it is the one that leaked 999 blocks under the rule as first written.

function main()
    s: str @ "ab"
    s @ s
    print(s)
    s @ s + s
    print(s)
    out: str @ ""
    i: int @ 0
    while i < 50
        out @ out + to_str(i % 10)
        i @ i + 1
    print(len(out))
    print(slice(out, from: 0, to: 10))
